    "description": "Social customs in Iran include forms of courtesy, reciprocal visiting, hospitality, gift exchange and ceremonies marking family events. Their meanings depend on relationships and setting, while regional studies reveal important differences within the country.",
    "tags": ["Social customs", "Taarof", "Visiting", "Gift exchange", "Weddings", "Regional variation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:39:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:39:52Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Customs in Iran: Courtesy, Visits and Family Occasions",
      "description": "Explore taarof, reciprocal visits, hospitality, gifts and wedding customs through scholarship that distinguishes historical accounts and regional differences."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-customs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Customs express relationships",
        "content": "Social customs give relationships a visible form: who is welcomed, who receives precedence, how an invitation is offered and how a gift is acknowledged. In Iran, scholarly accounts repeatedly connect these actions with courtesy and social standing. Their significance lies in the relationship between participants, rather than in an isolated gesture. An offer of a seat, for example, can acknowledge another person’s importance. [s1]\n\nIt is helpful to distinguish several overlapping subjects. Hospitality concerns receiving and caring for guests. Visiting customs organize encounters between households. Gift exchange extends relationships through material gestures. Taarof concerns the language and behavior through which people offer respect, minimize their own claims and negotiate generosity. These practices intersect, but none explains every social encounter. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "courtesy-and-taarof",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Taarof and the meaning of an offer",
        "content": "*Taarof* is often translated as ritual politeness, although no short English expression captures its full range. Anthropologist William O. Beeman describes both verbal forms of deference and gestures such as giving another person precedence through a doorway. Respect can be conveyed by elevating the person addressed while presenting oneself modestly. [s1]\n\nThe Global Informality Project emphasizes that scholars do not share a single definition of taarof. Its examples encompass invitations, compliments, offers and refusals. Such exchanges can maintain relationships, but they can also introduce pressure or ambiguity. The same outwardly generous gesture may therefore need interpretation within a particular conversation. [s3]\n\nFor an international reader, this is a reason to attend to context rather than memorize a fixed number of refusals. Describing taarof as simple dishonesty misses the social work performed by courteous language; assuming that every offer must be accepted literally misses its possible ambiguity. Neither reading adequately describes the range documented by researchers. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "visiting-and-reciprocity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Visits, refreshments and reciprocal attention",
        "content": "The Persian expression *did-o bazdid* refers to exchanging visits. Iranica’s historical account discusses greetings, leave-taking, congratulations and condolences alongside visits between friends and relatives. It also records refreshments and reciprocal gifts as elements of maintaining social ties. [s2]\n\nThese descriptions need a date and a setting. The article draws extensively on historical travelers and distinguishes more traditional social milieus. Its accounts of formal seating, dress or sequences of service should consequently be read as evidence about particular conventions, rather than instructions applicable to every contemporary home. The historical record helps explain the vocabulary and significance of a visit without supplying a universal household rulebook. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "family-occasions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Wedding celebrations and regional differences",
        "content": "Wedding customs illustrate why a broad overview must leave room for local distinctions. The *aqd* is the marriage-contract ceremony, while *arusi* denotes the wedding celebration. A scholarly account distinguishes these stages and describes regional differences in the *sofreh-ye aqd*, the ceremonial spread of symbolic objects. The distinction is useful for understanding a historical description or an invitation; the article is not a statement of current marriage law. [s4]\n\nResearch on Gilan, the Caspian region in northern Iran, offers a more specific view. Christian Bromberger describes marriage as an alliance involving gifts and counter-gifts between families. His discussion relates these exchanges to property and kinship, demonstrating that ceremonial giving can have substantial social and economic dimensions. It should not be reduced to a decorative accessory of the wedding. [s5]\n\nThe same regional study describes marriage strategies that diversify social connections rather than simply reinforcing close kin groups. This is a useful corrective to treating one model of “the Iranian family” as nationally uniform. Local evidence can complicate a generalization as well as illustrate it. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-food",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Food connects everyday and ceremonial life",
        "content": "UNESCO’s account of flatbread making and sharing offers another perspective on social customs. It describes cooperation in preparation, participation by relatives and neighbors, and the sharing of bread at both ordinary meals and significant occasions. Iran is one of the countries included in this multinational heritage element. [s6]\n\nIts importance for this overview is the connection between practical work and social belonging. Customs involve preparing, serving and sharing, as well as the words spoken around a table. UNESCO’s heritage description documents the meaning attributed to a recognized practice; it does not measure how frequently every Iranian household participates. Read together, regional research, historical accounts and studies of interaction provide a richer guide than a single list of national manners. [s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
